#THIS IS FILE IMPMAIN, THE C main OF AN IMP72 PROGRAM, IN IMP (IT WAS C, LIB/IMPMAIN.C): THE main PROGRAM OF THE SOURCE (THE STATEMENTS BEFORE ITS FIRST SUBR) IS THE FUNCTION impmain.  IT IS IN A LIBRARY MEMBER
 OF ITS OWN SO THAT A PROGRAM WITH A main OF ITS OWN DOES NOT LINK THIS ONE; SUCH A PROGRAM CALLS imp_init(argc, argv) AND imp_run() ITSELF (LIB/IMPINIT.IML, LIB/IMPRUN.IML, LIB/IMP.H), AND THIS main IS THOSE TWO CALLS.  THE C LIBRARY CALLS main WITH ARGC
 AND ARGV BY VALUE; AN IMP ROUTINE TAKES ITS ARGUMENTS BY REFERENCE, SO THE VALUES ARE THE ADDRESSES IT IS GIVEN: LOC(ARGC) IS ARGC AND LOC(ARGV) IS ARGV, AND CCALL PASSES THEM ON AS VALUES #
SUBR main(ARGC,ARGV) IS (
 CCALL imp_init(LOC(ARGC),LOC(ARGV));
 CCALL imp_run(0);
 0)
%%
